A||N||21||Ozzy||Coupe to a GT???||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||02-27-2000||11:59 PM||||Your thoughts and opinions are welcomed and appreciated.<P>I have an '88 Coupe. Body in good condition, Red, Interior in good condition and most of the steering and suspension rebuilt. Also has 5 speed in very good shape. Brakes are rebuilt etc, etc, etc! I payed too much for it I feel, (3k) but knew nothing of these little 'addictions' at that time. So...knowing that when I grow up to be a BIG Fiero boy, I'm gonna wan't a GT, here is what I would like your thoughts on.<P>What if I convert the Coupe to a GT? Clips and panels don't seem that hard to find or that expensive. I could then maybe get a 3.4L or 3.1L. After all, if I'm going to the trouble to transplant, I don't want the stock 2.8L. I can work on the dash and interior stuff later. I also have the power windows and door locks from the '84 SE. (Cheapy I bought recently)<P>I know, I know, I've vowed and declared that I would not do a transplant ever! I also had in my mind that I would find a GT after I buy a house and our first child is born yada, yada, yada, but I got to thinking today. I have yet to see an '88 GT for less than $5,500 around here and have seen them up to $9,500. I already have the '88 body, suspension, steering, brakes etc. Even if I found a GT it's gonna need work right?? (It's a Fiero, remember??) Not to mention for me it's learning a completly new car. I figure at least another $1,000 on a GT that I would probably pay around $5,500 for anyway for fixing what aint workin'.<P>I'm already into my Coupe for $3,000 + about $900 in parts. (Forget the hours!!!) I reckon I'd be lucky to sell it for $3,000. Probably end up closer to $2,500 so.......First, can I use this 5 speed for a 2.8L, 3.1L, 3.4L??? If so, then I see it this way: <P>If I take the 1500 bucks I'd lose on selling the Coupe' + the $3000 to make $5,500 for an '88 GT + the $1,000 to fix that GT...The way my math works that gives me some $5,500 to spend on my possible conversion and have a Fiero that I know every bloody square inch of and not be out of pocket (Depending on your cash flow perspective). Not to mention I could probably put the DIS 'duke' with the old ECM in the 84 SE, as it still is a recently rebuilt strong engine, and put all of the clean coupe clips, panels, etc on the SE (just so happens it's the same red!)and sell it for a coupla bucks along with the old '84 duke, ECM, blah, blah, blah!<P>SOunds like I've almost convinced myself! (The missus is next!!) Not really...she want's a GT as bad as I do. What I need is your experience and ideas here to see if this is worth it or not. It's a ton of bloody work and I don't have the cash to put into all of it right now but........<P><BR>Damn! Did not realize how long winded I got. Sorry 'bout that.<BR>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 02-28-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Shiner||02-28-2000||12:09 AM||||You are right, it's going to be a TON of work, and the car will be off the road for quite some time.  Personally, I am kind of against notchback to fastback conversions.  The coupe you have now sounds like a very nice car.  I would keep that, and get an '87 GT.  Much cheaper than an '88, and you can make up for the differences in the suspension later.  You have to like the car for what it is, you know what I mean?<P> Whatever you do, good luck!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||02-28-2000||06:13 AM||||I'll leave the coupe to fastback conversion alone.  I would suggest that, instead of swapping to a 3.1/3.2/3.4 you think about a 3800SC conversion.  It will be a little more expensive, but should be a much better motor.  I still want a V8 (like the Batmobile) but I'm considering a 3800 in a couple of years.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||02-28-2000||07:27 AM||||I've heard said that the change-over from a 4 to 6 cyl is not the easiest thing to do, also the 88GT(and Formula)do have some suspension differences from the coupes. Sounds like it would be more cost efficient to get an 86-87 fastback and fix it up. Some people say you can use the 4cly 5sp with a V6 but I would be hesitant. Glad I could make your day||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ozzy||02-28-2000||08:20 PM||||What are the suspension differences?<P>What 5 spd is in the GT? And what 5 spd do I have in my Coupe?<P>I don't mind the work part so much...Can I do it is more the point.<P>Ozzy||||reg||5
Z||000005||Monkeyman||02-28-2000||08:33 PM||||The 5-spd in the GT is a Getrag.  The one in the coupe is probably an Isuzu.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||02-29-2000||01:04 AM||||Yep, the 5 speed in the GT is a Getrag, the 5 speed in the coupe is an Isuzu.  GM says the Isuzu isn't rated to handle the torque the v6 puts out, but they had warranty claims to consider.  It should be fine if you don't flog it.<P>The only difference between the coupe and GT/Formula suspension in 88 is that the GT and Formula came with rear sway bars, sfaik.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||02-29-2000||05:50 AM||||"sfaik"????||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ozzy||02-29-2000||07:52 PM||||yeah..."sfaik"??||||reg||5
Z||000009||Phil||02-29-2000||09:22 PM||||So far as I know||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||03-01-2000||12:30 AM||||yep  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 03-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ozzy||03-01-2000||02:01 PM||||OIC.RU4REEL???<P>So what about the panels, clips etc. Do they bolt straight on. Do I have to get them from an '88 GT or Formula or whatever or will other years work too?<P>Would the Isuzu tranny bolt direct to the 2.8L or maybe a 3.4L or do I need a bell housing adapter?<P>Will a complete cradle from a GT/Formula bolt into the Coupe so I dont have to mess with engine mounts and such. <P>Is the engine compartment the same dimensions or is there cutting and structural changes necessary?<P>Is the radiator different. Larger cooling tubes??<P>Do I need a physciatrist...am I ready for "the room with the rubber wallpaper"!!!?<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000012||SCCA FIERO||03-01-2000||02:40 PM||||yes, but you need the strut bolts for the 88's to hold the grills. No, you can get them from any year fastback.<P>Yes it bolts up right to it (any tranny from a Fiero wil bolt to any motor that came in a Fiero (auto or manual)<P>Yes it would, but would be cheaper/easier to get the mounts.<P>Nope<P>Nope. I have the radiator that came in my 88 Coupe running my V6 and the stock tubes. I'm sure they are all the same so Pontiac wouldn't have to fab up 2 different sets of pipes.<P>I would have to say... YES!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ozzy||03-01-2000||04:37 PM||||Thnx SCCA Fiero! I'm not sure which way 2 go at this point. I may well need to be slapped in the "I luv me Jacket" but following my earlier logic, I may well do this.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000014||SCCA FIERO||03-01-2000||06:43 PM||||I plan to do what you want to.... almost. I have the fastback clip and everything I need to do the conversion, but the clip is black and my car is red. I also have the sunroof skin and everything I need to do that too, but the s/r skin is a different shade of red than my car. I hope to get sidescoops, decklid scoop and a scoop or vent for my hood then put the f/b clip and s/r skin on then get it painted. I already have a V6 in my Coupe and have GT wheels on it.<P>I guess I'm gonna have an 88 Coupe, fastback, GT hybrid?<P>I like notchbacks, but the fastback has graceful lines and an alomost exotic look. I have seen a Formula with a fastback on it and I like it! ||||reg||12
Z||000015||SCCA FIERO||03-01-2000||06:53 PM||||If you are worried about the Isuzu not holding up to your V6... I have the Isuzu in my 88 Coupe with an 86 2.8 V6 strapped to it and have dogged the hell out of it. Once I left a couple black marks about 5 or 6 feet long from a 3K dump... no trouble. I autocross my car and in small parking lots there are many times to chirp second... no problems. I am not saying it will last forever or that yours will not have problems. Of course I have a 5spd Getrag and a M17 4spd sitting out in my shed "just in case" something happens to the Isuzu. I also plan on buying another Isuzu from a guy I know for $75 including cables... can't pass that up.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||87SC/GT||03-01-2000||11:17 PM||||I think Ryane Motorsports has a kit to pop a 88' cradle into a 84-87 car.Might be easier to locate a 86-87GT and take that route. I bought my 87 GT 5 spd for 350.00! It was of course, the less desireable 2-3 piece cam model.  ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ozzy||03-02-2000||12:17 AM||||My Coupe is already and '88.<P>Damn...wish I could find an '87 GT for $350.00. Why don't they have bargains on the west coast.<P>Hey SCCA... Did you transplant the 6cyl into your Coupe? Also, you mention putting in a sunroof. Did your coupe not have one. Mine does already...or moon roof as I think they are called. With the different tail lights on a GT, does the decklid have to change too??||||reg||1
Z||000018||SCCA FIERO||03-02-2000||02:28 AM||||Yes I did put a V6 in place of the tired Iron Duke. I had an 86SE with the V6 in it that I owned for many years. The body/frame had seen better days but the motor and tranny were fine. I had the complete car and the process was not very hard. I think it took me a week to do it all. I documented everything on my website  <A HREF="http://msnhomepages.talkcity.com/RallyRd/scca_fiero/" TARGET=_blank>http://msnhomepages.talkcity.com/RallyRd/scca_fiero/</A>  if you're thinking of doing it.<P>My Fiero does not have the sunroof but I have all the parts to convert it.<P>To switch to the fastback clip you do need the decklid and tail lights. You also need the rear wireing harness for the GT lights.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Ozzy||03-02-2000||11:17 AM||||Cool dude!  You have been very helpfull. I going to have to see how the money goes for now. <P>The best thing would be if I could find a wrecked GT that got smashed in front but they seem pretty hard to find out here.<P>I looked around on the web last night for parts. Lottsa guys on the forum like, SuperX, FieroDave etc seem to have stuff but they all live so bloody far away. Seems all the cheap deals are in the mid northern and east part of the country.<P>Of course I'm in rainy ol' Washinton state. <P>Any ideas, anyone, on what is involved in buying cars from Canada?? Taxes, titles etc.<P>I see some nice deals in Vancouver, Canada. Not too far from here.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000021||87SC/GT||03-02-2000||07:30 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Ozzy:<BR>[B]"My Coupe is already and '88." <BR>I'm just lazy.<P>"Damn...wish I could find an '87 GT for $350.00. Why don't they have bargains on the west coast."<P>I paid 350.00, The poor dude that had it repo'd will be paying the 4500.00 judgement for years.Good luck with the switch!J<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Ozzy||03-02-2000||11:36 PM||||Hey SCCA... nice web page!!!  You almost make it sound easy.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
